  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03170v1</id>
    <title>Beyond Cons: Purely Relational Data Structures</title>
    <updated>2025-10-03T16:44:03Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03170v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.03170v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We present {Kanren} (read: set-Kanren), an extension to miniKanren with constraints for reasoning about sets and association lists. {Kanren} includes first-class set objects, a functionally complete family of set-theoretic constraints (including membership, union, and disjointedness), and new constraints for reasoning about association lists with shadowing and scoped lookup. These additions allow programmers to describe collections declaratively and lazily, without relying on structural encodings and eager search over representation spaces. The result is improved expressiveness and operational behavior in programs that manipulate abstract data -- particularly interpreters -- by supporting set equality based on contents, enabling finite failure. We describe the design and implementation of {Kanren} in a constraint-enabled miniKanren system and illustrate its use in representative examples.</summary>
    <category term="cs.PL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-10-03T16:44:03Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>17 pages, 6 figures, Source code available at https://www.github.com/rvs314/faster-clpset-minikanren . To be published in the 7th Workshop on miniKanren and Relational Programming (miniKanren'25)</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.PL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Rafaello Sanna</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>William E. Byrd</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Nada Amin</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
